Function Types:
Kotlin let's you treat a Function as an Object too. But you will have to know the Type.
A Function Type tells you what type of Data it can take in and give out.
Syntax:
(Input Data Type)->Output Data Type
There are multiple Function Types:
1.()->Unit:-Input is nothing and Output is also nothing i.e. No Input is passed and no output is not returned.
2.(Int)->Unit:-Input is passed but Output is not returned.
3.(String,String)->Unit:-Takes two Input but doesn't return any Output.
4.()->Object:-No input is not passed but an Object is Returned.
5.(String)->Name:-Input is Passed and an Object is Returned.
6.(String)->String:-Input is passed and Output is Returned and not object.
7.(String,String)->String:-Multiple Input is passed and Output is Returned.
Predicate: (T)->Boolean:-Asks as Yes No Question.
Transformation: (T)->R:-Converts one data to another object or datatype.
Operation: (T)->:Does some operation and gives no output.
A Function Type can be used anywhere you would normally use a datatype and they can also contain other functions.
Parameter can be fuctions and optional ones. It let's you pass inn Custom Hook for starting or finishing something without any change in Main Code.
It can be used in Classes and List.
You can also Nest them inside each other.
All this shows their flexibility.
When many parameters are used in a function type, then it suggests a Parameter Name. It places a name before the parameter and separate them using commas. They are not neccesary but are used for Programmers Convinenince.
Type Alias is used to give a nickname the long Function so that it is easier to call. Using it helps our codes look cleaner. During the Compilation, Kotlin Swaps the Type Alias for function.
Type Alias is also used when two different function has the same name so we can use it to call them differently and avoid clash.
All Function types are interfaces. And that is why a class can use it.